```mdl
decision D-118: The llms.txt pages move from the Agents groups to each section's Resources group, HALO gets one, and the footer drops its "For agents" column. `/halo/llms.txt`, `/plex/llms.txt` and their `llms-full.txt` are generated from the spec like ECHO's, so PLEX's file is no longer a placeholder. The old `/docs/echo/agents/llms-txt/` and `/docs/plex/agents/llms-txt/` redirect. Amends [D-116] and [D-117].
  date: 2026-11-11
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-6.1, F-6.3, echo-llms-txt, halo-llms-txt, plex-llms-txt]
```

```mdl
decision D-119: The snippet sources are read from disk with `fs`, not imported through `import.meta.glob` with `?raw`. In dev, Astro collects a page's CSS by walking its imports, and it follows each `?raw` import into the real module's graph, so the first render of `/echo` compiled the tutorial app, the demos and their theme before it answered (about 11 s).
  date: 2026-11-11
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-3.6]
```

```mdl
decision D-120: The docs footer's Docs column lists ECHO, HALO and PLEX, in the topic switcher's order, each linking its section's Overview; the Composer entry goes. Fills in [F-7].
  date: 2026-11-11
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-7]
```

```mdl
decision D-121: The Overview hero crops its background from the left and keeps the right edge, where the mark sits, so a narrow content column never cuts the mark off, and its heading never breaks. It stacks the art behind the text only on a phone; on wider windows it gets room from [D-123], not from a stacked layout.
  date: 2026-11-11
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-16.8]
```

```mdl
decision D-122: Each `/echo` demo shows a loading placeholder in its frame until it hydrates. The placeholder is static markup in the page, so it costs nothing to compile and does not name the demo, whose title lives in the demo's own module.
  date: 2026-11-11
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-7.8]
```

```mdl
decision D-123: Docs pages favor room for code. The table of contents folds into the bar above the page until the window fits the sidebar, a full text column and the table of contents (88rem), as the Effect docs do, and code samples no longer wrap: a long line scrolls sideways.
  date: 2026-11-11
  by: user:wittjosiah
  supersedes: D-17
  affects: [F-2.5, F-2.14, T-4]
  why: Wrapped code is hard to read, since a wrapped line looks like two statements. A text column squeezed by the table of contents wraps or cuts off code that would otherwise fit.
```

```mdl
decision D-124: The docs sidebar folds behind the menu button below 72rem, the width that fits the sidebar and a full text column, instead of Starlight's 50rem, as the Effect docs do. Fills in [D-123].
  date: 2026-11-11
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-2.15]
  why: Between 50rem and 72rem the sidebar took a third of the window and left the code samples too narrow.
```

```mdl
decision D-125: The docs' code samples are formatted to 80 columns, not the repository's 120, and the text column is 50rem (850px), the width that fits 80 columns of code inside a numbered step, beside the space Expressive Code keeps for its copy button. Fills in [D-123].
  date: 2026-11-11
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-2.5]
  why: The Effect docs aim for 80 columns: of about 3,000 code lines across 12 of their pages, 12 are longer. At 120 columns, 9% of our sample lines scrolled in the old 782px column. Comments are rewrapped by hand, since the formatter does not wrap them.
  rejected:
    - Keep 120 columns and widen the column to fit them: about 1,140px, which puts the body text far past a readable line length.
```

```mdl
decision D-126: PLEX's Start here group has Why PLEX? in place of Try Composer, like Why ECHO? and Why HALO?, and PLEX has a glossary in Resources like HALO's. `/docs/plex/try/` redirects to Why PLEX?. Fills in [D-90].
  date: 2026-11-11
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [why-plex, app-framework-glossary, app-framework-welcome]
  why: The three sections open the same way, so a reader moving between them finds the same pages in the same places.
```

```mdl
decision D-127: ECHO and PLEX have Build with a coding agent as the third page in Start here; HALO has none yet. PLEX's replaces Build a plugin with the assistant, whose route redirects to it. The page's prompt is a text box the reader edits before opening it in Claude Code (`claude://code/new?q=`), Codex (`codex://new?prompt=`) or Cursor (`cursor://anysphere.cursor-deeplink/prompt?text=`). PLEX's prompt starts with a fixed line that tells the agent to use the `composer-plugin-dev` skill, or to read it from the repository when it is not installed, since none of the three links can install a plugin. Its default text is the eval's World Clock brief without "test it yourself": the skill's default flow ends with the reader enabling the dev server, which needs no debug port.
  date: 2026-11-11
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-6.4, echo-build-with-agent, plex-build-with-agent, app-framework-welcome]
  why: The `composer-plugin-dev` eval shows the skill plus a one-line brief builds a working plugin in 5 to 7 minutes for about $0.50, and readers use more than one coding agent.
  rejected:
    - An install link per agent: Claude's links only prefill a prompt; Codex's plugin-install link takes a Codex marketplace, which the `dxos` plugin is not in.
```

```mdl
decision D-128: Agents may read a whole section in one fetch: `llms-full.txt` holds every page of it, examples included. A page must not assume an agent reads one page at a time, or that an example on one page is unknown to an agent reading another.
  date: 2026-11-11
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-6.1]
  why: The `composer-plugin-dev` eval's agent reads the plugin guide, whose World Clock example matches its brief, so the eval measures how well an agent follows the guide.
```

```mdl
decision D-129: The plugin guide keeps World Clock as its worked example, as the eval's guide-following case. New worked examples stay off the subjects the eval holds out, such as a habit tracker or a standup board; Bookmarks (PLEX learning path), Contacts (ECHO tutorial) and Notes (the Open in Claude prompt) are already taken. Check with the eval's owner before adding a plugin example.
  date: 2026-11-11
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [plex-build-with-agent]
  why: An example that matches a held-out brief lets the agent copy the answer, and the eval stops measuring what it is meant to.
```

```mdl
decision D-130: Build with a coding agent replaces the Open in Claude pages, and the Agents groups go with them; their routes redirect to the section's Build with a coding agent page. The fixed lines move out of view: the page shows only the text the reader edits. PLEX's fixed lines install the `composer-plugin-dev` skill into the folder, downloading `SKILL.md` and the files it links from the repository into the agent's own skills folder (`.claude/skills`, `.agents/skills` or `.cursor/skills`), then have the agent follow it; the copied prompt names all three folders. ECHO's fixed line has the agent read `/echo/llms-full.txt` first. The three open buttons are all primary, each in the palette color nearest its agent's brand: Claude Code in the property orange, Codex in the string blue, Cursor in the foreground neutral. The Notes example goes with the Open in Claude pages. Amends [D-116], [D-117] and [D-127].
  date: 2026-11-11
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-6.4, F-17.1, echo-build-with-agent, plex-build-with-agent]
  why: A reader should not need the `dxos` Claude Code plugin installed, or have to read a line they cannot change, and no one agent should look like the default.
  rejected:
    - Telling the agent to use the skill if installed and read it otherwise: it depends on the Claude Code plugin, and the other two agents have no way to install it.
```

```mdl
decision D-131: Each open button shows its agent's logo before its label, in the button's text color. The logos are Lobe Icons' monochrome SVGs (MIT), kept in `src/assets/agents/`. Amends [D-130].
  date: 2026-11-11
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-6.4]
  why: A reader finds their agent by its mark faster than by its name.
  rejected:
    - Simple Icons (CC0): it has the Claude and Cursor marks but dropped OpenAI's, and has no Codex mark.
```

```mdl
decision D-132: Build with a coding agent does not explain the buttons. Amends [D-130].
  date: 2026-11-11
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [echo-build-with-agent, plex-build-with-agent]
  why: Pressing a button shows the whole prompt, fixed lines included, before anything is sent.
```

```mdl
decision D-133: Build with a coding agent targets Composer Preview (`https://preview.composer.space`), not composer.space, which loads only the plugins it ships with. The Composer address moves out of the editable prompt into PLEX's fixed lines, after the skill step, and the page says to sign in to Composer Preview first. Amends [D-127].
  date: 2026-11-11
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [plex-build-with-agent]
  why: composer.space cannot load a plugin from a dev server, and a reader should not have to know which Composer to name.
```

```mdl
decision D-134: The PLEX page asks the reader to open an empty folder in their agent before pressing a button, and the prompt does not check the folder. Claude Code's link takes only an absolute `folder` path and Codex's an existing `path`; Cursor's takes none, and a web page cannot see the reader's files to fill one in. The skill sets the plugin up as its own repository with npm, so a folder inside a monorepo is not offered. Amends [D-130].
  date: 2026-11-11
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [plex-build-with-agent]
  why: One sentence of setup works the same in all three agents.
  rejected:
    - A prompt step that stops when the folder is not empty: the reader is told what to do, and the step adds a line they did not ask for.
    - A folder field that fills `folder` and `path`: the folder must already exist, Cursor ignores it, and typing an absolute path is more work than opening the folder.
    - Having the agent make the folder: Codex's sandbox and Claude Code's permissions block or prompt on writes outside the open folder.
```

```mdl
decision D-135: The reader may open any folder, not only an empty one. When it is not empty, PLEX's fixed lines have the agent make a new folder for the plugin inside it: where the project keeps its packages, such as `packages/` in a monorepo, set up with the project's package manager, or else a folder named after the plugin. Amends [D-134].
  date: 2026-11-11
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [plex-build-with-agent]
  why: A reader should be able to start from wherever their agent already is, and a new folder inside the open one stays within the agent's sandbox.
```

```mdl
decision D-136: The PLEX page assumes an empty folder again, and the fixed lines no longer have the agent make one. Placing a plugin inside an existing project, such as a monorepo, is follow-up work on the skill and this page. Reverts [D-135].
  date: 2026-11-11
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [plex-build-with-agent]
  why: The skill sets a plugin up as its own repository with npm; a monorepo needs the skill changed first.
```

```mdl
decision D-137: The learning path does not embed StackBlitz in its pages. The ECHO object shows the finished app as the in-page embed, with Fork on StackBlitz; Installation, where the reader starts writing code, has Follow along in StackBlitz, which opens a starter with the learning path's code taken out. Both open in their own tab through the SDK's `openProject`, with files the docs build generates, so the docs need no isolation headers. To make the starter possible, each page's code moves from the standalone scripts in `src/tutorial/steps/` into the module of the app that runs it.
  date: 2026-11-11
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-15.6, F-15.7, F-15.8, F-15.9, the-echo-object, installation, defining-types, querying, mutating-objects, sharing]
  why: An inline StackBlitz embed needs `Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy` and `Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: require-corp` on the docs page, which restricts everything else the page loads, and in a 2026-11-11 test it ran only in WebKit; Chrome blocked its preview relay and Firefox's preview stayed blank.
  rejected:
    - A StackBlitz embed on every learning-path page that carries the reader's files from page to page: the isolation headers and browser support above.
    - A StackBlitz project from a GitHub folder: the starter would be a second committed copy of the app.
```

```mdl
decision D-138: The StackBlitz buttons post a form to `stackblitz.com/run`, the request the SDK's `openProject` sends, with the project's files in the page. The starter replaces each export in a quoted region with `todo('<page>, step <n>: <step>')`, and the app shows the stage the written pieces reach under a line naming the next step. `@dxos/*` pins pkg.pr.new commit `6bad7b6` of dxos#14487, its only published build, until that PR lands. Fills in [D-137].
  date: 2026-11-11
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-15.7, F-15.8, F-15.9]
  why: A click that first fetched the files or loaded the SDK would open the tab after an await, which browsers block as a popup; the files are about 60 KB and only two pages carry them.
  rejected:
    - Adding `@stackblitz/sdk` for one form post.
```

```mdl
decision D-139: Each StackBlitz button shows StackBlitz's bolt logo before its label, in the button's text color. The logo is Simple Icons' SVG (CC0), kept in `src/assets/brands/`. Amends [D-138].
  date: 2026-11-11
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-15.7, F-15.8]
```

```mdl
decision D-140: The tutorial app is one device: it opens its own space, and from Sharing a space on its toolbar has Invite, which copies a code, and Join, which takes one and switches the app to that space. The name and the online switch belong to a device frame the docs draw around each copy of the app, not to the app. Every embed shows one device except Sharing a space and The ECHO object, which show Alice's and Bob's side by side, each with a space of its own; only Alice's is seeded. The StackBlitz projects run the app as a real client, with no frame: `client.ts` configures EDGE and keeps the database in the browser's private file system, so a second browser can join, and the app reopens its space after a reload. The embeds keep their in-memory clients. Amends [D-137].
  date: 2026-11-11
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-15.3, F-15.7, F-15.8, installation, sharing, the-echo-object]
  why: The embeds mixed the demo's controls into the app, so the finished app lacked sharing and the second peer opened on a join screen no real app has; the StackBlitz project is where a reader builds a real app, which an in-memory network is not.
  rejected:
    - In-memory devices in StackBlitz too, which cannot share with anything outside the page.
```

```mdl
decision D-141: The PLEX learning path ends each page in a plugin inspector, not in Composer. The inspector is a PLEX app on an in-memory client that loads the reader's plugin at its stage, with one panel per kind of contribution; the docs embed it, StackBlitz runs it, and the plugin's dev server serves it.
  date: 2026-11-11
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-19, F-20, app-framework-create-plugin, app-framework-define-type, app-framework-add-surface, app-framework-add-operation, app-framework-extend-app-graph]
  why: Seeing a step land in Composer needs an account, Composer Preview and a dev server from the first page; the inspector shows each contribution on its own, in the page, and the reader keeps it after the docs.
  rejected:
    - A cut-down Composer in the embed, which hides most contributions behind navigation and needs the whole app to boot.
```

```mdl
decision D-142: The PLEX plugin is the path's first page: what a plugin is, the finished Bookmarks plugin in the inspector, and Fork on StackBlitz, as The ECHO object opens ECHO's path, so Create a plugin no longer shows it running. Follow along in StackBlitz is on Create a plugin.
  date: 2026-11-11
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [plex-the-plex-plugin, app-framework-create-plugin, F-20.3]
```

```mdl
decision D-143: Composer comes in on Run it in Composer, the page before Publishing: Composer Preview, the dev server, the command palette and the assistant. Create a plugin no longer loads the plugin into a local Composer, and Publishing no longer covers developing against one.
  date: 2026-11-11
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [plex-run-in-composer, app-framework-create-plugin, app-framework-add-operation, app-framework-publishing]
```

```mdl
decision D-144: PLEX learning-path pages may use Effect where the PLEX API is Effect's, such as an operation handler written as `Effect.gen`. ECHO's pages stay with the promise APIs.
  date: 2026-11-11
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [app-framework-add-operation, F-20]
```

```mdl
decision D-145: Installation documents every piece of `vite.config.ts` that ECHO needs in the browser: `vite-plugin-wasm` in the page and in `worker.plugins`, `build.target: 'esnext'`, `optimizeDeps.exclude` for `@dxos/wa-sqlite`, `@automerge/automerge` and `@automerge/automerge-subduction`, and `optimizeDeps.include` for the CommonJS modules only the database worker imports. The icon sprite's dist scan and the Tailwind source for `@dxos` stylesheets stay in the StackBlitz template without explanation, since they serve react-ui, which the docs use only for demonstration.
  date: 2026-11-11
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [installation]
```

```mdl
decision D-146: The PLEX learning path has no StackBlitz projects: The PLEX plugin has no Fork on StackBlitz and Create a plugin has no Follow along in StackBlitz. The reader builds the plugin in a local package, and the inspector drops the starter placeholder. Amends [D-141] and [D-142].
  date: 2026-11-11
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-19, F-20, plex-the-plex-plugin, app-framework-create-plugin]
  why: A plugin running in StackBlitz is served from StackBlitz's machine, where neither the docs inspector nor Composer can load it, so the reader would end up with a plugin they cannot carry into Run it in Composer.
```

```mdl
decision D-147: The inspector is a module workbench: a header with the plugin, its source and the sample object; a sidebar of modules with their status; and a main area showing the selected module working. Learning-path pages load the reader's own plugin from its dev server with Load dev plugin, and check it against what the page adds; only The PLEX plugin loads the finished plugin by itself. The dev server no longer serves the inspector. Amends [D-141].
  date: 2026-11-11
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-19, F-20, plex-the-plex-plugin, app-framework-create-plugin, app-framework-define-type, app-framework-add-surface, app-framework-add-operation, app-framework-extend-app-graph, plex-run-in-composer]
  why: Tabs per kind of contribution, filled from the docs' own copy of the plugin, did not show the reader what their own code did; the path adds one module per page, so the modules are the natural index.
  rejected:
    - A mini Composer, with a nav tree, an article and a command palette: it hides translations, schema and skills, and shows nothing until the app graph page.
    - Serving the inspector from the plugin's dev server: the docs inspector reaches the dev server already.
```

```mdl
decision D-148: PLEX's Core concepts are Plugins and modules, Capabilities, Activation, Operations, Surfaces and App Graph, in that order. Anatomy of a plugin becomes Plugins and modules at `/docs/plex/concepts/plugins/`, and `/docs/plex/introduction/` redirects to it.
  date: 2026-11-11
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [app-framework-anatomy, plex-capabilities, plex-activation, app-framework-operations, app-framework-surfaces, app-framework-app-graph]
  why: Anatomy of a plugin packed capabilities and activation into one page and still described the removed plugin context; a reader meets operations before surfaces on the learning path, so the concepts follow that order.
```

```mdl
decision D-149: "App Graph" is a proper name in PLEX prose: two words, capitalized, without "the". The namespace stays `AppGraph`, and the learning-path page is Extend the App Graph at the same route.
  date: 2026-11-11
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [app-framework-app-graph, app-framework-guide-app-graph, app-framework-extend-app-graph, app-framework-glossary]
  why: It names one structure in the app, like ECHO or HALO, not a kind of graph.
```

```mdl
decision D-150: PLEX's API group adds PluginManager and Translations. AppGraph covers `AppNode` and `GraphPath`, and AppCapabilities is a tour in groups with one table, not a section per capability.
  date: 2026-11-11
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [plex-guide-plugin-manager, plex-guide-translations, app-framework-guide-app-graph, app-framework-guide-app-capabilities]
  why: A reader loading plugins at runtime or writing strings had no page; AppCapabilities has too many members for one heading each.
```

```mdl
decision D-151: PLEX's Guides are Using ECHO in a plugin, Testing a plugin, Settings and state, Effect in plugins, Debugging a plugin and React. Using ECHO in a plugin is a guide, not a concept, because it joins ECHO and PLEX. The dx CLI stays later.
  date: 2026-11-11
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [plex-guide-using-echo, plex-guide-testing, plex-guide-settings-and-state, plex-guide-effect, plex-guide-debugging, app-framework-guide-react, app-framework-cli]
  why: Each answers a task a plugin author hits after the learning path; registering ECHO types is integration work, not an idea of PLEX's own.
  rejected:
    - Objects in the app as a core concept: it is not a PLEX concept.
```

```mdl
decision D-152: The PLEX API reference covers `@dxos/app-framework`, `@dxos/app-toolkit`, `@dxos/app-graph` and `@dxos/compute`. Operation is documented from `@dxos/compute/Operation`, since `@dxos/operation` exports only the invoker.
  date: 2026-11-11
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [app-framework-api-reference, app-framework-guide-operation]
  why: Those are the packages a plugin imports from.
```

```mdl
decision D-153: The plugin manifest is written, in Resources, at `/docs/plex/resources/manifest/`. Concept and API snippets come from the Bookmarks modules, except Plugin's, which keep World Clock [D-129], and concept pages may embed the inspector with the finished plugin.
  date: 2026-11-11
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [app-framework-manifest, app-framework-operations, app-framework-surfaces, app-framework-app-graph]
  why: One plugin carried from the learning path into the reference lets a reader recognize each snippet.

decision D-154: ECHO's Core concepts are Types and schema, Objects and relations, References and DXNs, Feeds, Queries, Spaces and databases, and Changes and branches, in that order. Text and blobs leaves the site map.
  date: 2026-11-11
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [types, objects-relations, references, feeds, queries, spaces, changes, text-blobs]
  why: A reader needs types before objects, and objects before how they are queried, stored and changed.

decision D-155: Versions and migrations are a section of Types and schema. Schema evolution stays later until the docs can say when migrations run outside a plugin.
  date: 2026-11-11
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [types, schema-evolution]
  why: A version belongs to the type; a page of its own would promise more than the docs can say yet.

decision D-156: Spaces and databases covers storage, replication and the Hypergraph, not joining. Access is HALO's, built on Keyhive.
  date: 2026-11-11
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [spaces]
  why: HALO's Invitations page owns the handshake; ECHO links to it.

decision D-157: ECHO's API group has thirteen namespace pages at `/docs/echo/api/<namespace>/`: Type, Annotation, Obj, Relation, Ref, Database, Query, Filter, Feed, Text, Blob, Migration and Tag. The old `/docs/echo/guides/<namespace>/` routes redirect. The other namespaces appear only in the generated reference.
  date: 2026-11-11
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [guide-type, guide-annotation, guide-obj, guide-relation, guide-ref, guide-database, guide-query, guide-filter, guide-feed, guide-text, guide-blob, guide-migration, guide-tag]
  why: API pages are reference, so they get their own route, apart from the guides.

decision D-158: ECHO's concept and API pages use the promise APIs. Effect is taught only in the Effect guide.
  date: 2026-11-11
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [guide-effect]
  why: Most readers start without Effect; one page teaches it for those who want it.

decision D-159: ECHO's Guides are Effect, Testing and React, with React last. Solid and Foreign records stay later, and Using ECHO in a plugin belongs to PLEX.
  date: 2026-11-11
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [guide-effect, guide-testing, guide-react, guide-solid, guide-foreign-records]
  why: These are the guides the docs can back with tested code today.

decision D-160: Concept and API snippets use the tutorial's Person, Organization and Event types. Relation snippets add a WorksAt relation without changing the tutorial app. Queries and Changes and branches may embed the contacts app.
  date: 2026-11-11
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [objects-relations, queries, changes]
  why: One set of types from the learning path lets a reader recognize each snippet.

decision D-161: Concurrency and conflicts becomes Changes and branches at `/docs/echo/concepts/changes/`, taking in history and branches. `/docs/echo/concepts/conflicts/` redirects.
  date: 2026-11-11
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [changes, conflicts]
  why: Merging, history and branches are one model: changes on a CRDT document.

decision D-162: HALO's pages teach `@dxos/halo` in Effect, with no promise form, and run it with `Effect.runPromise`. ECHO's promise-only rule [D-158] does not apply to HALO.
  date: 2026-11-11
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [halo-guide-identity, halo-guide-invitation, halo-guide-space, halo-guide-passkey-recovery]
  why: `@dxos/halo` is Effect-only; a promise wrapper the package does not ship would teach an API that does not exist.

decision D-163: HALO describes Keyhive in the present tense, inside each concept page, with no Keyhive page of its own. Code stays agnostic of what runs underneath.
  date: 2026-11-11
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [halo-welcome, halo-identities-devices, halo-spaces-membership, halo-invitations, halo-recovery-credentials]
  why: Keyhive is the model the API is shaped around; the docs describe the model, and the code does not change when the implementation does.

decision D-164: HALO's core concepts are Identities and devices, Spaces and membership, Invitations, and Recovery and credentials, in that order.
  date: 2026-11-11
